# Single worker process: a free workaround for the rate limiter's
# per-worker counters (see RATE_LIMIT_STORAGE_URI in app.py) — gunicorn
# threads share one process's memory, so memory:// storage is accurate
# again with --workers 1, at the cost of losing multi-core parallelism
# for CPU-bound plan computation. Once a real shared store (Redis) is
# provisioned, set RATE_LIMIT_STORAGE_URI/REDIS_URL and raise --workers
# back up — the limiter code already supports that without further
# changes.
#
# 8 threads: matches what's actually live on Render's free tier
# (0.1 vCPU / 512MB, confirmed via the Render API's get_metrics/
# get_service -- see the 2026-09-22 usage audit). This file previously
# said "24 threads... sized for Render's Standard tier" while the
# service was actually deployed on the free tier with a manual dashboard
# Start Command override reading 8 threads -- i.e. this file was
# describing infrastructure that didn't exist, and had no effect either
# way since the dashboard override wins. Kept in sync with the dashboard
# now; if you ever remove that override so Render reads this file again,
# bump the thread count here first only alongside a deliberate paid-tier
# upgrade (not before -- see project cost-sensitivity notes), not on its
# own. Threads help here despite the GIL because each request's dominant
# cost is I/O wait (the Groq/Ollama HTTP call, Supabase reads) -- a
# thread blocked on a network call releases the GIL, so other threads
# still run.
web: gunicorn --workers 1 --threads 8 --timeout 60 --bind 0.0.0.0:$PORT app:app
